# What the Reader should expose for word-columns.pdf, written from what the
# document means. Every paragraph and cell starts with its place in the reading
# order, [1] to [26]: a single-column introduction, two columns, a table across
# both, two columns again, and a closing paragraph.
#
# Language (#49): the text is English, but the PDF's catalog says /Lang (ja) —
# set from the editing settings of the Word that exported it. Word declares en
# on every run's Span, and every run with letters agrees, so the document is
# taken to be English: the article is marked en, and so is the heading at the
# top, which is the document's /Title. The headings, paragraphs and cells are
# in the article's language and carry no lang of their own. The catalog's ja is
# still what the document information panel shows. The tab's title cannot
# carry a language of its own, and stays in the interface's.
#
#   - The table's first column is row headers: Word's "Table Grid" style has
#     "First Column" on, and Word tags those cells TH.
#   - Every table sits in a region named 表, so it can scroll by keyboard.
document lang=ja title="Reading order across columns（リーダー） — Accessible PDF View"
  banner
    heading level=1 lang=en "Reading order across columns"
  main
    region "リーダー"
      article lang=en
        heading level=2 "[1] Reading order across columns"
        paragraph "[2] Each paragraph starts with its place in the reading order. Two columns follow, then a table across both columns, then two columns again."
        heading level=2 "[3] Left column"
        paragraph "[4] The first paragraph of the left column. This sentence is here to give the paragraph some length, so that the column holds several lines of text and its position on the page is unambiguous."
        paragraph "[5] The second paragraph of the left column. This sentence is here to give the paragraph some length, so that the column holds several lines of text and its position on the page is unambiguous."
        heading level=2 "[6] Right column"
        paragraph "[7] The first paragraph of the right column. This sentence is here to give the paragraph some length, so that the column holds several lines of text and its position on the page is unambiguous."
        paragraph "[8] The second paragraph of the right column. This sentence is here to give the paragraph some length, so that the column holds several lines of text and its position on the page is unambiguous."
        heading level=2 "[9] A table across both columns"
        region "表"
          table
            row
              columnheader "[10] Step"
              columnheader "[11] What happens"
              columnheader "[12] Where it is"
            row
              rowheader "[13] One"
              cell "[14] The table is read after the right column."
              cell "[15] Across both columns"
            row
              rowheader "[16] Two"
              cell "[17] Each row is read left to right."
              cell "[18] Across both columns"
            row
              rowheader "[19] Three"
              cell "[20] The text after the table comes next."
              cell "[21] Across both columns"
        heading level=2 "[22] Left column again"
        paragraph "[23] The left column after the table. This sentence is here to give the paragraph some length, so that the column holds several lines of text and its position on the page is unambiguous."
        heading level=2 "[24] Right column again"
        paragraph "[25] The right column after the table. This sentence is here to give the paragraph some length, so that the column holds several lines of text and its position on the page is unambiguous."
        paragraph "[26] The last paragraph, after the columns."
